WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.380
An obstruction changes what happens upstream.

00:00:03.100 --> 00:00:06.220
In this urinary tract
illustration, urine normally moves from

00:00:06.220 --> 00:00:08.780
the renal pelvis through
the ureter toward the bladder.

00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:11.417
Now a narrowing interrupts that outflow.

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:15.880
The supplied chapter distinguishes
hydroureter, dilation of the ureter,

00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:19.793
from hydronephrosis, dilation of
the renal pelvis and calyces.

00:00:20.260 --> 00:00:23.456
Watch the collecting spaces
expand upstream of the obstruction.

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:28.660
Severity depends on its
location, completeness, duration, and whether

00:00:28.660 --> 00:00:31.107
one or both upper tracts are involved.

00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:34.703
Prolonged damage can produce
fibrosis and loss of nephrons.

00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:38.573
When obstruction is relieved,
increased urine output may follow.

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:43.100
The chapter calls this
postobstructive diuresis and warns of

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:44.687
fluid and electrolyte imbalance.

00:00:45.340 --> 00:00:48.110
This film does not supply the missing explanation of

00:00:48.220 --> 00:00:50.572
how unilateral obstruction causes hypertension.

00:00:51.460 --> 00:00:54.980
That requested guide component
remains explicitly marked as a

00:00:55.020 --> 00:00:55.627
textbook gap.
